{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01L3dwMqEu6ShktF3UmNvy1Y","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The user wants me to generate detailed predicted experimental data for all \"planned\" experiments in the experiment plan, under the \"best\" tier scenario. Each prediction needs a result_rationale explaining why this result would occur under this tier.\n\nLet me carefully go through each planned experiment and generate realistic, internally consistent predictions.\n\nKey best-tier parameters:\n- delta_over_base_liberoplus_sr_pp: [8, 15]\n- a3_gap_full_vs_no_conformal_pp: [5, 10]\n\n\n- a4_gap_perDOF_vs_scalar_pp: [3, 6]\n- coc_success_spearman_rho: [0.5, 0.7]\n- aci_ellipsoid_volume_narrowing_pct: [20, 40]\n- srpo_stacking_gain_pp: [3, 6]\n- srpo_stacked_absolute_sr: [85, 88]\n- libero_nominal_degradation_pp: [0, 1]\n- crm_per_perturbation_gain_camera_noise_robotstate_pp: [10, 20]\n\nNow I'm working through the parameter ranges for the best-tier scenario to establish realistic bounds for each metric.\n\nLet me think through plan_001 with the ACR-VLA full pipeline. The base VLA achieves around 97% on LIBERO nominal with minimal degradation when ACR is applied, but on LIBERO-Plus the baseline drops significantly—SRPO reaches 82.1% as the state-of-the-art, so the base model without that training would be considerably lower, probably around 60%, and ACR-VLA should push that up by roughly 11 percentage points to around 71%.\n\nFor the nominal benchmark, I'm estimating base performance at 97.2% with ACR-VLA dropping just 0.4 percentage points to 96.8%, staying well within the acceptable degradation range.\n\nPlan_002 tests the A3 ablation by removing conformal features entirely, leaving only the observation-conditioned residual component. That should cost us about 7-8 percentage points, bringing performance down to roughly 66.5%—this is the critical test for whether the conformal features actually matter.\n\nPlan_003 is examining the A4 ablation around scalar uncertainty...\n\nFor the scalar uncertainty variant, I'm expecting a 4-5 percentage point hit, landing around 69.5%. Then looking at SRPO stacking, the base sits at 82.1% and stacking should add 3-6 points, so I'm targeting around 86.4%. The COC signal validation shows Spearman correlation in the 0.5-0.7 range with convergence happening within about 50 episodes and ellipsoid volume narrowing by roughly 30%. Finally, the A1 ablation removes ACI entirely, relying only on static calibration—that should cost 2-3 points, putting us around 71.5%.\n\nNow I'm working through the remaining ablations. Without the learned correction module in A2, we're left with just sample selection like enhanced ReconVLA, which gives us maybe 4 points of gain on the base for around 66%. Breaking down the perturbation effects, camera, noise, and robot state drive the biggest improvements at 10-20 points, while lighting and background contribute 5-10 points, and layout plus language are honest limitations at just 0-3 points. For the statistical testing approach, Bonferroni is more conservative and loses the cross-DOF structure that CGS captures, so CGS should be worth 2-3 points. Finally, comparing COC's per-step updates against episode-level ACI updates, the finer-grained per-step signal should give COC the edge.\n\nMoving to the uncertainty comparison, DiU's aleatoric features capture within-distribution variance but don't help much under distribution shift, whereas CGS's epistemic uncertainty should outperform it by 3-5 points, with the combined 52D features adding only marginal gains of around 1 point. Looking at the gradient analysis, I can see distinct attention patterns across different perturbations—camera shifts activate the wrist rotation DOFs most, noise spreads attention uniformly, and robot state changes focus on the position DOFs. For the SnapFlow integration, using K=10 with single-step sampling gives us roughly an 8x speedup compared to the K=10 with 100-step DDPM approach while maintaining sample diversity.\n\nNow I'm setting up the baseline numbers and establishing consistency across the performance metrics. I need to anchor everything to SmolVLA's nominal LIBERO performance at 97.2% and the LIBERO-Plus average at 62.3%, then work through the deltas for ACR-VLA and other variants to ensure the numbers align properly. Looking at the per-perturbation results for plan_008, I'm seeing gains across different perturbation types—camera and noise showing the strongest improvements around 15-18pp, while layout and language have minimal impact at 3-4pp. The average across all seven perturbations comes to about 10.4pp, though I should be careful about directly comparing this to the overall L3-L5 average since those might involve combined perturbations rather than individual ones. Let me refocus on the key plans—plan_006 uses static calibration without online ACI adaptation, which is a useful baseline to understand how much the adaptive component contributes. Finishing the COC comparison—it hits 74.1% while episode-level drops to 72.6%, so there's a 1.5pp advantage to the finer-grained approach. For the aleatoric versus epistemic uncertainty, DiU's aleatoric features only get to 67.4% since they capture noise but miss the distributional shift, whereas CGS epistemic reaches 74.1%, and combining both gives a marginal 0.7pp boost to 74.8%. I'm also looking at feature importance through gradient analysis and checking SnapFlow's distillation performance—it trades 1.6pp accuracy for an 8.4x speedup. Now I want to verify that the per-perturbation gains actually align with what I'd expect before writing this up as clean YAML.\n\nLooking at the base SmolVLA numbers across LIBERO-Plus, the gains make sense—camera and noise perturbations start lowest and benefit most from synthetic augmentation, while language and layout are already strong and see minimal improvement. This confirms the approach is working where it matters most.\n\nNow I'm mapping out what visualizations each experiment needs: main results and success curves for the baseline, ablation rows across multiple plans, stacking comparisons, scatter plots for the COC analysis, per-perturbation breakdowns with bar charts and heatmaps, uncertainty comparisons, feature importance analysis, and speed-accuracy tradeoffs. Let me compile the full YAML structure with all these outputs.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11112,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11112},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3a8fe5df-a524-42fe-ad30-68007c28aa51","uuid":"f2e12011-6805-4582-9a25-63b9528b29a4"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01L3dwMqEu6ShktF3UmNvy1Y","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"```yaml\ntier: best\nnarrative_guidance: >\n  Original framing holds intact. Headline: \"ACR-VLA achieves dimension-selective action\n  correction via per-DOF conformal features, improving base VLA by 11.8% on LIBERO-Plus\n  and pushing SRPO SOTA from 82.1% to 86.4%.\" All three claims supported with clear\n  ablation evidence. Per-perturbation breakdown shows largest gains on camera/noise/robot-state\n  (coverable by synthetic augmentation), honest limitation on layout/language. Feature\n  importance analysis shows CRM attends to different DOF uncertainty dimensions under\n  different perturbation types — strong mechanistic evidence for dimension-selective\n  correction. Position as first method to use structured multivariate CP statistics\n  as learned correction features.\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: \"ACR-VLA Full Pipeline (SmolVLA base)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_001\"\n    description: >\n      End-to-end ACR-VLA: CGS (K=10, Ledoit-Wolf) extracts 45D per-DOF conformal features\n      from flow-matching samples, ACI+COC adapts conformal threshold online, CRM (131K params)\n      outputs dimension-selective action correction. CRM trained on LIBERO demos + 5 synthetic\n      augmentation types (camera jitter, Gaussian noise, robot-state offset, lighting shift,\n      background swap). Evaluated on LIBERO (10 tasks × 20 rollouts) and LIBERO-Plus L3–L5\n      (7 perturbation types × 5 tasks × 20 rollouts).\n    expected_results:\n      libero_nominal_sr_base: 97.2\n      libero_nominal_sr_acrvla: 96.8\n      libero_nominal_degradation_pp: 0.4\n      liberoplus_l3l5_sr_base: 62.3\n      liberoplus_l3l5_sr_acrvla: 74.1\n      delta_over_base_liberoplus_sr_pp: 11.8\n      crm_inference_overhead_ms: 3.2\n      total_inference_time_ms: 385\n      cgs_covariance_condition_number_median: 14.7\n      cgs_ledoit_shrinkage_intensity_mean: 0.38\n    result_rationale: >\n      K=10 Ledoit-Wolf covariance at p/n=0.7 retains sufficient cross-DOF structure because\n      shrinkage intensity ~0.38 strikes the bias-variance sweet spot — enough regularization\n      to prevent rank deficiency while preserving the dominant eigenvectors that encode\n      meaningful DOF covariance (validated in MVP where K=10 degraded only ~15pp vs oracle).\n      Flow-matching samples at K=10 capture meaningful epistemic uncertainty because the\n      learned ODE solver's stochasticity at different initial conditions spans the model's\n      posterior predictive — under distribution shift (camera/noise/robot-state perturbations\n      covered by synthetic augmentation), sample spread along affected DOFs increases\n      monotonically with perturbation severity. CRM's 131K-param capacity is sufficient\n      because the task is low-dimensional regression (45D→7D residual) with strong inductive\n      bias from the conformal feature structure; overfitting is controlled by the feature\n      bottleneck rather than requiring larger capacity. The 11.8pp gain lands in the upper\n      half of the [8,15] range because synthetic augmentation covers 5 of 7 perturbation\n      types well, and the two uncovered types (layout/language) have smaller base failure\n      rates, limiting the dilution of average gains. Nominal degradation is minimal (0.4pp)\n      because CRM learns near-zero residuals when conformal features indicate low uncertainty\n      (in-distribution), effectively self-gating.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 1: Main Results on LIBERO and LIBERO-Plus\"\n        columns: [\"Method\", \"LIBERO (nominal) SR%\", \"LIBERO-Plus L3 SR%\", \"LIBERO-Plus L4 SR%\", \"LIBERO-Plus L5 SR%\", \"L3-L5 Avg SR%\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"SmolVLA (base)\", 97.2, 71.8, 60.4, 54.7, 62.3]\n          - [\"SmolVLA + ReconVLA\", 96.5, 75.2, 64.8, 59.1, 66.4]\n          - [\"SmolVLA + ACR-VLA (ours)\", 96.8, 82.6, 72.3, 67.4, 74.1]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 2: Success rate vs perturbation level (L1–L5) curves for base SmolVLA, ReconVLA, and ACR-VLA. ACR-VLA curve degrades more gracefully, maintaining >65% at L5 where base drops below 55%.\"\n      - \"Fig 3: Example rollout visualization comparing base VLA (fails at grasp due to noisy wrist rotation) vs ACR-VLA (CRM corrects wrist DOFs 5-6 while preserving position DOFs 1-3), with overlaid conformal ellipsoid cross-sections.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The 11.8pp improvement validates the core thesis that structured per-DOF conformal\n      features enable meaningful action correction. The near-zero nominal degradation (0.4pp)\n      confirms CRM learns to be inactive when uncertainty is low. Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage\n      intensity averaging 0.38 indicates moderate regularization — the covariance is neither\n      collapsed to diagonal (which would lose cross-DOF information) nor unregularized\n      (which would be unstable at p/n=0.7). Condition numbers in the teens indicate\n      well-conditioned estimation. Per-level breakdown shows gains increase with perturbation\n      severity (L3: +10.8, L4: +11.9, L5: +12.7), consistent with epistemic uncertainty\n      being more informative under stronger shifts.\n\n  - name: \"Ablation A3 — CRM without Conformal Features\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_002\"\n    description: >\n      CRM receives only the current observation embedding and base action as input (no 45D\n      conformal features). Architecture identical to full CRM but input dimension reduced\n      from 45+obs_dim to obs_dim. This is equivalent to an A2C2-style generic observation-\n      conditioned residual policy. Same training data and procedure as plan_001.\n    expected_results:\n      liberoplus_l3l5_sr_a3: 66.8\n      a3_gap_full_vs_no_conformal_pp: 7.3\n      libero_nominal_sr_a3: 96.4\n    result_rationale: >\n      Without conformal features, CRM can only learn a generic observation-conditioned\n      residual — it knows something is wrong (via visual mismatch in the observation) but\n      not which action dimensions are unreliable or how they co-vary. This forces it to\n      apply uniform correction across all DOFs, which helps on average but sometimes\n      degrades already-correct dimensions. The 7.3pp gap (well within the [5,10] make-or-\n      break range) arises because conformal features provide two critical pieces of information\n      the observation alone cannot: (1) per-DOF uncertainty magnitude, allowing selective\n      correction intensity, and (2) cross-DOF covariance structure, allowing coordinated\n      multi-joint corrections. The A3 variant still outperforms the no-correction base by\n      4.5pp because the observation embedding carries some perturbation-relevant signal, but\n      this improvement is substantially less efficient than the conformal-conditioned version.\n      Nominal performance degrades slightly more (0.8pp vs 0.4pp) because without uncertainty\n      gating, CRM occasionally applies spurious corrections in-distribution.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 2 (row): Ablation A3 in main ablation table\"\n        columns: [\"Variant\", \"Conformal Features\", \"CRM\", \"ACI\", \"LIBERO SR%\", \"L-Plus L3-L5 SR%\", \"Δ vs Full\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"A3: No conformal\", \"✗\", \"✓\", \"✓\", 96.4, 66.8, \"-7.3\"]\n          - [\"Full ACR-VLA\", \"✓\", \"✓\", \"✓\", 96.8, 74.1, \"—\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Ablation bar chart (Fig 5a): side-by-side bars for Full vs A3 across L3/L4/L5, showing the gap widens at higher perturbation levels (L3: -5.8, L4: -7.6, L5: -8.5).\"\n    analysis: >\n      The 7.3pp gap is the strongest evidence for Claim 1. It demonstrates that conformal\n      features are not merely redundant with observation information — they provide a\n      complementary uncertainty signal that the observation encoder alone cannot extract.\n      The widening gap at higher perturbation levels (L5 gap: 8.5pp) confirms that conformal\n      features become increasingly valuable as distribution shift intensifies, consistent\n      with epistemic uncertainty scaling with shift magnitude. This ablation cleanly\n      separates \"knowing what to correct\" (conformal features) from \"being able to correct\"\n      (CRM capacity), showing both are necessary.\n\n  - name: \"Ablation A4 — Scalar Uncertainty (ReconVLA-style)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_003\"\n    description: >\n      CRM receives scalar uncertainty features instead of 45D per-DOF features. Scalar\n      features: mean Euclidean pairwise distance among K=10 samples (1D), mean sample std\n      (1D), maximum sample std (1D) — total 3D uncertainty input, matching ReconVLA's\n      information granularity. Same CRM architecture scaled for 3D uncertainty input.\n    expected_results:\n      liberoplus_l3l5_sr_a4: 69.7\n      a4_gap_perDOF_vs_scalar_pp: 4.4\n      libero_nominal_sr_a4: 96.6\n    result_rationale: >\n      Scalar uncertainty tells CRM \"how uncertain the model is overall\" but not \"which DOFs\n      are uncertain.\" This forces CRM to learn an average correction profile rather than\n      dimension-selective adjustment. The 4.4pp gap (within [3,6]) reflects the information\n      loss from collapsing 7-DOF covariance structure into scalars. Specifically, scalar\n      features cannot distinguish between (a) high uncertainty in wrist rotation only (correct\n      fix: adjust DOFs 5-6-7) and (b) high uncertainty in position only (correct fix: adjust\n      DOFs 1-2-3) — both map to the same scalar value. The scalar variant still outperforms\n      A3 (no conformal) by 2.9pp because even aggregate uncertainty magnitude is useful for\n      scaling correction intensity. The gap is moderate rather than large because many\n      perturbation types affect multiple DOFs simultaneously, partially reducing the\n      advantage of per-DOF resolution. The per-DOF advantage is expected to be most\n      pronounced on perturbations that selectively affect specific DOF subsets (e.g.,\n      camera perturbation primarily affects position inference while sparing gripper).\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 2 (row): Ablation A4\"\n        columns: [\"Variant\", \"Uncertainty Dim\", \"LIBERO SR%\", \"L-Plus L3-L5 SR%\", \"Δ vs Full\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"A4: Scalar (3D)\", \"3\", 96.6, 69.7, \"-4.4\"]\n          - [\"Full ACR-VLA (45D)\", \"45\", 96.8, 74.1, \"—\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 5b: Per-perturbation-type gap between Full and A4. Largest gap on camera (+6.2pp) and robot-state (+5.8pp) perturbations where DOF-selectivity matters most; smallest on noise (+2.1pp) which affects all DOFs uniformly.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The scalar-to-perDOF upgrade yields 4.4pp, confirming that structured uncertainty is\n      strictly more informative than aggregate uncertainty. Combined with A3 (7.3pp gap for\n      any-uncertainty-vs-none), we see a clear hierarchy: per-DOF (74.1) > scalar (69.7) >\n      none (66.8) > base (62.3). This ordering is the central empirical result supporting\n      Claim 1 and validates the core motivation for multivariate conformal prediction over\n      scalar approaches.\n\n  - name: \"SRPO Stacking\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_004\"\n    description: >\n      ACR-VLA applied on top of SRPO-finetuned SmolVLA. New CRM trained on SRPO checkpoint\n      using same procedure (LIBERO demos + synthetic augmentation). CGS calibration set\n      re-computed from SRPO checkpoint's flow-matching samples. Evaluated on LIBERO-Plus\n      L3-L5.\n    expected_results:\n      srpo_base_sr: 82.1\n      srpo_plus_acrvla_sr: 86.4\n      srpo_stacking_gain_pp: 4.3\n      libero_nominal_srpo_acrvla: 97.8\n      libero_nominal_srpo_base: 98.1\n      nominal_degradation_pp: 0.3\n    result_rationale: >\n      SRPO improves the base policy's training-time robustness by optimizing for perturbation\n      invariance, but residual errors remain — particularly on out-of-distribution perturbation\n      combinations and severity levels beyond training coverage. ACR-VLA operates at inference\n      time, exploiting the complementary signal that SRPO's flow-matching samples still\n      exhibit elevated epistemic uncertainty on these residual failure modes. The 4.3pp gain\n      (within [3,6]) is smaller than the 11.8pp gain on vanilla SmolVLA because SRPO has\n      already addressed the \"easy\" failures (perturbations well-covered by training-time\n      augmentation), leaving harder cases where CRM correction is less reliable. The stacked\n      86.4% (within [85,88]) represents new SOTA on LIBERO-Plus. The gain is non-trivial\n      because ACR-VLA and SRPO address complementary failure modes: SRPO reduces the frequency\n      of high-uncertainty predictions (training-time), while ACR-VLA corrects the remaining\n      high-uncertainty predictions (inference-time). Nominal degradation is minimal because\n      SRPO's already-robust in-distribution performance leaves little room for CRM to\n      interfere.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 3: Stacking Results\"\n        columns: [\"Method\", \"Training-time\", \"Inference-time\", \"LIBERO SR%\", \"L-Plus L3-L5 SR%\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"SmolVLA\", \"—\", \"—\", 97.2, 62.3]\n          - [\"SmolVLA + ACR-VLA\", \"—\", \"ACR-VLA\", 96.8, 74.1]\n          - [\"SRPO\", \"SRPO\", \"—\", 98.1, 82.1]\n          - [\"SRPO + ACR-VLA\", \"SRPO\", \"ACR-VLA\", 97.8, 86.4]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 6: 2×2 matrix plot showing training-time (SRPO vs none) × inference-time (ACR-VLA vs none). Both axes contribute, and gains are roughly additive (11.8 + 4.3 ≈ expected if orthogonal), confirming complementarity.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The 4.3pp stacking gain is the headline number for Claim 3: inference-time conformal\n      correction provides value orthogonal to training-time optimization. The approximate\n      additivity (base→ACR: +11.8, SRPO→SRPO+ACR: +4.3, sum 16.1 vs actual 24.1 total\n      improvement from base to SRPO+ACR) suggests partial but substantial complementarity.\n      The new SOTA of 86.4% pushes LIBERO-Plus performance into a regime where the\n      remaining failures are dominated by layout/language perturbations that neither\n      synthetic augmentation nor training-time optimization can fully address.\n\n  - name: \"COC Signal Validation\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_005\"\n    description: >\n      Three analyses: (a) Spearman rank correlation between per-episode COC coverage\n      fraction (fraction of timesteps where chunk-overlap conformal prediction set contains\n      the next observed action) and binary task success. (b) ACI convergence plot: running\n      conformal threshold α_t over episodes. (c) Ellipsoid volume comparison: ACI-adapted\n      vs static calibration at matched marginal coverage (90%).\n    expected_results:\n      coc_success_spearman_rho: 0.62\n      coc_success_spearman_p_value: 0.00003\n      aci_convergence_episodes: 45\n      aci_final_alpha_std_across_tasks: 0.018\n      aci_ellipsoid_volume_narrowing_pct: 31\n      aci_marginal_coverage_achieved: 0.904\n      static_marginal_coverage_achieved: 0.897\n      aci_mean_ellipsoid_volume: 0.043\n      static_mean_ellipsoid_volume: 0.062\n    result_rationale: >\n      COC achieves ρ=0.62 (within [0.5,0.7]) because chunk overlap is a reliable proxy for\n      prediction quality: when the model's predictions at overlapping chunk positions are\n      self-consistent, the action trajectory is likely coherent and on-track; inconsistency\n      signals the model is uncertain about the trajectory, which correlates with failure.\n      The correlation is strong but not perfect because (a) some failures are due to\n      execution errors in correct predictions, and (b) some successes occur despite moderate\n      COC violations (the robot recovers from brief uncertainty). ACI converges in ~45\n      episodes because the step-level COC signal provides dense feedback (~200 updates per\n      episode × 45 episodes ≈ 9000 gradient steps on α), much faster than episode-level\n      binary signal. The 31% ellipsoid volume narrowing (within [20,40]) occurs because ACI\n      tightens the threshold under in-distribution conditions (where static calibration is\n      conservative) while loosening it under shift (where static calibration is too tight),\n      resulting in better-calibrated, tighter prediction sets on average. This is the\n      standard ACI advantage: adaptive thresholds converge to the optimal per-context\n      threshold rather than using a single global threshold.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 4: COC Validation Metrics\"\n        columns: [\"Metric\", \"Value\", \"95% CI\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"COC-Success Spearman ρ\", 0.62, \"[0.51, 0.72]\"]\n          - [\"ACI convergence (episodes to |α_t - α*| < 0.01)\", 45, \"[38, 55]\"]\n          - [\"Ellipsoid volume (ACI)\", 0.043, \"[0.038, 0.049]\"]\n          - [\"Ellipsoid volume (static)\", 0.062, \"[0.055, 0.070]\"]\n          - [\"Volume narrowing\", \"31%\", \"[22%, 38%]\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 4a: Scatter plot of per-episode COC coverage fraction vs task success (binary), with logistic regression fit and Spearman ρ annotation. Clear separation between success/failure clusters with COC coverage threshold ~0.75.\"\n      - \"Fig 4b: ACI convergence plot — running α_t over 200 episodes, showing convergence to stable value by episode ~45. Shaded region shows ±1 std across 5 seeds.\"\n      - \"Fig 4c: Box plot comparing ellipsoid volumes under ACI vs static calibration at matched 90% coverage. ACI distribution is tighter and shifted left (smaller volumes).\"\n    analysis: >\n      ρ=0.62 validates COC as a self-referential progress signal — no external supervision\n      needed. The convergence speed (~45 episodes) is practical for online deployment.\n      The 31% volume narrowing demonstrates ACI's value: at the same coverage guarantee,\n      ACI produces tighter conformal sets, which in turn give CRM more precise uncertainty\n      features. This validates Claim 2's core proposition that self-referential ACI\n      maintains calibrated uncertainty without external supervision.\n\n  - name: \"Ablation A1 — No ACI (Static Calibration)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_006\"\n    description: >\n      ACR-VLA with static conformal threshold (calibrated once on calibration set, never\n      updated). CGS and CRM identical to full pipeline. Isolates ACI online adaptation\n      contribution.\n    expected_results:\n      liberoplus_l3l5_sr_a1: 71.2\n      a1_gap_vs_full_pp: 2.9\n      libero_nominal_sr_a1: 96.9\n    result_rationale: >\n      Without ACI, the conformal threshold is fixed at calibration-set level. Under\n      distribution shift, this static threshold is miscalibrated — typically too loose\n      (calibrated on in-distribution data where uncertainty is lower), causing conformal\n      sets to be too large and conformal features to overestimate uncertainty. CRM trained\n      on well-calibrated features during training then receives inflated uncertainty signals\n      at test time, leading to over-correction. The 2.9pp gap is moderate because CRM has\n      some robustness to feature distribution shift (it learned a smooth mapping), but the\n      systematic bias in uncertainty overestimation degrades precision. The gap is not\n      catastrophic because CGS's structural information (which DOFs are uncertain, cross-DOF\n      covariance) is still informative even with a miscalibrated scale. ACI's primary\n      contribution is recalibrating the scale, not changing the structure.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 2 (row): Ablation A1\"\n        columns: [\"Variant\", \"ACI\", \"LIBERO SR%\", \"L-Plus SR%\", \"Δ vs Full\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"A1: Static calibration\", \"✗\", 96.9, 71.2, \"-2.9\"]\n    figures_description: []\n    analysis: >\n      The 2.9pp ACI contribution is meaningful but secondary to the conformal features\n      themselves (7.3pp A3 gap) and per-DOF structure (4.4pp A4 gap). This ordering\n      (features > granularity > adaptation) supports the paper's emphasis on per-DOF\n      conformal features as the primary contribution, with ACI as a valuable but not\n      essential enhancement.\n\n  - name: \"Ablation A2 — ACI without CRM (Sample Selection Only)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_007\"\n    description: >\n      CGS + COC + ACI used for multivariate-CP-guided sample selection (select the sample\n      closest to the conformal set centroid), but no learned CRM correction. Extends\n      ReconVLA's selection paradigm to multivariate CP. Isolates CRM contribution.\n    expected_results:\n      liberoplus_l3l5_sr_a2: 66.5\n      a2_gap_vs_full_pp: 7.6\n      libero_nominal_sr_a2: 97.0\n    result_rationale: >\n      Sample selection alone (even with multivariate CP guidance) is fundamentally limited\n      because it can only choose among the K=10 existing samples — it cannot synthesize an\n      action better than any individual sample. When all K samples are biased in the same\n      direction (common under distribution shift), selection cannot correct the bias. CRM's\n      learned residual can extrapolate beyond the sample set. The 7.6pp gap quantifies the\n      value of learned correction over pure selection. The A2 variant still improves over\n      base by 4.2pp because multivariate CP-guided selection outperforms random/mean\n      aggregation: it identifies which sample is most consistent with the conformal set\n      geometry, effectively performing soft outlier rejection. This improvement is comparable\n      to ReconVLA's reported gains, validating the multivariate extension. Nominal\n      performance is slightly better than full ACR-VLA (97.0 vs 96.8) because sample\n      selection is more conservative than learned correction in-distribution.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 2 (row): Ablation A2\"\n        columns: [\"Variant\", \"CRM\", \"LIBERO SR%\", \"L-Plus SR%\", \"Δ vs Full\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"A2: Selection only\", \"✗\", 97.0, 66.5, \"-7.6\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Comparison bar in Fig 5a showing A2 (selection) vs A3 (no conformal) at similar levels (~66-67%), demonstrating that without CRM, even structured selection provides limited advantage over observation-conditioned residual.\"\n    analysis: >\n      A2 and A3 perform similarly (~66.5 vs 66.8%), revealing an interesting symmetry:\n      knowing what's uncertain without being able to correct it (A2) is roughly as valuable\n      as being able to correct without knowing what's uncertain (A3). Full ACR-VLA's 7+pp\n      advantage over both confirms the synergy is super-additive — CRM needs conformal\n      features to know where to correct, and conformal features need CRM to act on the\n      information.\n\n  - name: \"Per-Perturbation Breakdown\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_008\"\n    description: >\n      ACR-VLA gain decomposed across all 7 LIBERO-Plus perturbation types at L4 severity.\n      20 rollouts × 5 tasks per perturbation type. Reports both base and ACR-VLA SR per\n      perturbation.\n    expected_results:\n      perturbation_gains:\n        camera:\n          base_sr: 52.0\n          acrvla_sr: 67.2\n          gain_pp: 15.2\n        noise:\n          base_sr: 46.0\n          acrvla_sr: 63.8\n          gain_pp: 17.8\n        robot_state:\n          base_sr: 55.0\n          acrvla_sr: 69.1\n          gain_pp: 14.1\n        light:\n          base_sr: 62.0\n          acrvla_sr: 73.3\n          gain_pp: 11.3\n        background:\n          base_sr: 66.0\n          acrvla_sr: 75.4\n          gain_pp: 9.4\n        layout:\n          base_sr: 72.0\n          acrvla_sr: 75.2\n          gain_pp: 3.2\n        language:\n          base_sr: 74.0\n          acrvla_sr: 75.8\n          gain_pp: 1.8\n      crm_per_perturbation_gain_camera_noise_robotstate_pp_avg: 15.7\n    result_rationale: >\n      Camera/noise/robot-state show the largest gains (14-18pp, within [10,20]) because\n      these perturbation types are well-covered by synthetic augmentation during CRM\n      training AND produce distinctive per-DOF uncertainty signatures that CRM can exploit.\n      Camera perturbation primarily inflates uncertainty in end-effector position DOFs\n      (DOFs 1-3) while leaving rotation/gripper relatively unaffected — CRM learns to\n      selectively correct position. Noise affects all DOFs but with characteristic\n      covariance patterns that CRM recognizes from training. Robot-state offsets produce\n      systematic biases in specific DOFs that CRM compensates via learned residuals.\n      Light/background show moderate gains (9-11pp) because they affect visual features\n      globally, producing less DOF-selective uncertainty patterns — CRM still helps but\n      the correction is more diffuse. Layout and language show minimal gains (2-3pp)\n      because (a) layout changes are not covered by synthetic augmentation, and (b)\n      language perturbations affect task semantics rather than action execution, producing\n      uncertainty patterns CRM never saw during training. This honest limitation is\n      intrinsic: no inference-time action correction can fix misunderstood instructions.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 5: Per-Perturbation Breakdown (L4)\"\n        columns: [\"Perturbation\", \"Base SR%\", \"ACR-VLA SR%\", \"Gain (pp)\", \"Covered by Synth Aug?\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Camera\", 52.0, 67.2, \"+15.2\", \"✓\"]\n          - [\"Noise\", 46.0, 63.8, \"+17.8\", \"✓\"]\n          - [\"Robot-state\", 55.0, 69.1, \"+14.1\", \"✓\"]\n          - [\"Light\", 62.0, 73.3, \"+11.3\", \"✓\"]\n          - [\"Background\", 66.0, 75.4, \"+9.4\", \"✓\"]\n          - [\"Layout\", 72.0, 75.2, \"+3.2\", \"✗\"]\n          - [\"Language\", 74.0, 75.8, \"+1.8\", \"✗\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 7a: Grouped bar chart (base vs ACR-VLA) across 7 perturbation types, sorted by gain. Clear visual separation between augmentation-covered (left, large gains) and uncovered (right, small gains) perturbation types.\"\n      - \"Fig 7b: Gain magnitude vs synthetic augmentation coverage annotation, supporting the honest limitation narrative.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The per-perturbation breakdown provides the most nuanced evidence for the paper's\n      narrative. The strong correlation between synthetic augmentation coverage and gain\n      magnitude (ρ≈0.9 across 7 types) is both a strength (mechanistic explanation) and\n      an honest limitation (layout/language resistance). Average gain on covered types\n      (camera/noise/robot-state) is 15.7pp, well within the [10,20] best-tier range.\n      This breakdown should be prominently featured to preempt reviewer concerns about\n      cherry-picked aggregate numbers.\n\n  - name: \"Ablation A5 — CGS vs Bonferroni\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_009\"\n    description: >\n      Replace joint Mahalanobis-based CGS (which uses full covariance) with per-DOF\n      Bonferroni-corrected marginal conformal prediction (treats each DOF independently,\n      applies α/7 per DOF). Same CRM architecture, 45D features but covariance-derived\n      features (off-diagonal ellipsoid axes) are uninformative.\n    expected_results:\n      liberoplus_l3l5_sr_bonferroni: 72.0\n      cgs_vs_bonferroni_gap_pp: 2.1\n    result_rationale: >\n      Bonferroni correction treats DOFs independently, discarding cross-DOF covariance\n      structure. This loses two types of information: (1) which DOF pairs co-vary under\n      uncertainty (e.g., shoulder pitch and elbow pitch are coupled), and (2) the ellipsoid\n      orientation that indicates the principal axes of uncertainty. The 2.1pp gap is modest\n      because per-DOF marginal features (std, marginal conformal radius) still capture most\n      of the variance — cross-DOF structure provides incremental but real value. Bonferroni\n      also produces overly conservative individual intervals (α/7 is much smaller than\n      needed when DOFs are correlated), inflating conformal radii and reducing CRM's ability\n      to discriminate truly uncertain from merely correlated DOFs.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 2 (row): Ablation A5\"\n        columns: [\"Variant\", \"CP Method\", \"L-Plus SR%\", \"Δ vs Full\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"A5: Bonferroni\", \"Marginal + correction\", 72.0, \"-2.1\"]\n          - [\"Full: CGS\", \"Joint Mahalanobis\", 74.1, \"—\"]\n    figures_description: []\n    analysis: >\n      The 2.1pp CGS advantage validates the use of multivariate CP over marginal Bonferroni\n      but is not a massive effect. This is expected — most of the per-DOF advantage comes\n      from having per-DOF resolution at all (4.4pp A4 gap), not from the specific covariance\n      structure. Reviewers may probe this; the response is that CGS provides a theoretically\n      principled joint guarantee while Bonferroni is known to be conservative, and the\n      2.1pp gap represents a free lunch from using the correct statistical framework.\n\n  - name: \"Ablation A6 — COC vs Episode-Level ACI\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_010\"\n    description: >\n      Replace COC per-step signal with episode-level binary task success for ACI updates.\n      ACI threshold updates once per episode (success → tighten, failure → loosen) instead\n      of per-step via COC. Same CRM and CGS.\n    expected_results:\n      liberoplus_l3l5_sr_episode_aci: 72.6\n      coc_vs_episode_gap_pp: 1.5\n      aci_convergence_episodes_episode_level: 120\n    result_rationale: >\n      Episode-level binary signal is sparser (~1 update per 200 steps vs ~200 updates per\n      episode with COC) and noisier (success/failure is binary, COC coverage fraction is\n      continuous). This slows ACI convergence from ~45 to ~120 episodes and produces a\n      coarser threshold estimate. The 1.5pp gap is modest because both variants eventually\n      converge to similar thresholds — the difference is convergence speed and precision.\n      COC's advantage is primarily practical (faster adaptation in non-stationary settings)\n      rather than asymptotic. In a fixed-distribution evaluation, the gap would shrink\n      further given enough episodes.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 2 (row): Ablation A6\"\n        columns: [\"Variant\", \"ACI Signal\", \"Convergence (eps)\", \"L-Plus SR%\", \"Δ vs Full\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"A6: Episode-level ACI\", \"Binary success\", 120, 72.6, \"-1.5\"]\n          - [\"Full: COC ACI\", \"Per-step COC\", 45, 74.1, \"—\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Supplementary: ACI convergence curves for COC vs episode-level, showing 2.7× faster convergence with COC.\"\n    analysis: >\n      COC's per-step signal provides faster and more precise ACI convergence. The 1.5pp\n      practical advantage justifies COC's inclusion, though the paper should honestly note\n      that the asymptotic difference is smaller. The convergence speed advantage (45 vs 120\n      episodes) is the stronger argument — in non-stationary deployments where perturbation\n      types change over time, fast adaptation is critical.\n\n  - name: \"Ablation A7 — DiU Aleatoric vs CGS Epistemic\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_011\"\n    description: >\n      Three variants: (a) CRM with DiU single-pass aleatoric uncertainty features (7D per-DOF\n      aleatoric std), (b) CRM with CGS epistemic features (45D, full ACR-VLA), (c) CRM with\n      combined epistemic+aleatoric features (52D). Validates that epistemic uncertainty is\n      the correct signal for distribution shift correction.\n    expected_results:\n      liberoplus_l3l5_sr_diu_aleatoric: 67.4\n      liberoplus_l3l5_sr_cgs_epistemic: 74.1\n      liberoplus_l3l5_sr_combined_52d: 74.8\n      epistemic_vs_aleatoric_gap_pp: 6.7\n      combined_vs_epistemic_marginal_gain_pp: 0.7\n      libero_nominal_sr_diu: 95.8\n      libero_nominal_sr_combined: 96.6\n    result_rationale: >\n      DiU's aleatoric uncertainty captures inherent action stochasticity — it's high even\n      in-distribution (at decision boundaries, near contact transitions). This makes it a\n      poor discriminator for distribution shift: the model reports high aleatoric uncertainty\n      regardless of whether it's in-distribution or shifted. CRM trained on aleatoric\n      features over-corrects in-distribution (nominal degradation 1.4pp) and under-corrects\n      under shift (cannot distinguish shift-induced from inherent uncertainty). CGS epistemic\n      uncertainty, by contrast, is low in-distribution and elevates specifically under shift,\n      providing the causal signal CRM needs. The 6.7pp gap directly validates the paper's\n      theoretical motivation: epistemic uncertainty (what the model doesn't know) is the\n      correct conditioning signal for correction, not aleatoric uncertainty (inherent\n      stochasticity). The combined 52D variant gains only 0.7pp over epistemic alone,\n      confirming that aleatoric features are largely redundant given epistemic features —\n      the marginal information in aleatoric variance (contact transition timing) provides\n      minimal additional value for correction. This ablation provides the clearest\n      mechanistic evidence for the CGS design choice.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 6: Epistemic vs Aleatoric Uncertainty\"\n        columns: [\"Uncertainty Type\", \"Dim\", \"LIBERO SR%\", \"L-Plus SR%\", \"Δ vs CGS\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"DiU aleatoric\", 7, 95.8, 67.4, \"-6.7\"]\n          - [\"CGS epistemic\", 45, 96.8, 74.1, \"—\"]\n          - [\"Combined\", 52, 96.6, 74.8, \"+0.7\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 8: Uncertainty magnitude distributions (violin plots) for epistemic vs aleatoric features under in-distribution vs shifted conditions. Epistemic shows clear separation (low in-dist, high shifted); aleatoric shows high variance in both conditions with substantial overlap.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The epistemic-vs-aleatoric distinction is a clean, interpretable result that reviewers\n      will appreciate. The 6.7pp gap is large enough to be unambiguous, and the combined\n      variant's marginal 0.7pp gain confirms the theoretical prediction that aleatoric\n      information is orthogonal but not additive to epistemic features for correction\n      purposes. Recommend including the violin plot (Fig 8) as it provides the most\n      intuitive mechanistic explanation.\n\n  - name: \"Feature Importance Analysis\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_012\"\n    description: >\n      Compute CRM gradient magnitude w.r.t. each of the 45 uncertainty input features,\n      averaged over test episodes, broken down by perturbation type. Visualize as 45×7\n      heatmap (features × perturbation types). Validates dimension-selective correction\n      mechanism.\n    expected_results:\n      feature_importance_patterns:\n        camera:\n          high_gradient_features: [\"dof1_std\", \"dof2_std\", \"dof3_std\", \"dof1_conformal_r\", \"dof2_conformal_r\"]\n          dominant_dofs: [1, 2, 3]\n          interpretation: \"Camera perturbation primarily affects position inference; CRM attends to position DOF uncertainties\"\n        noise:\n          high_gradient_features: [\"ellipsoid_volume\", \"dof1_std\", \"dof4_std\", \"dof5_std\", \"dof6_std\"]\n          dominant_dofs: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]\n          interpretation: \"Noise affects all DOFs; CRM uses global ellipsoid volume + distributed per-DOF features\"\n        robot_state:\n          high_gradient_features: [\"dof1_conformal_r\", \"dof2_conformal_r\", \"dof3_conformal_r\", \"dof1_std\", \"dof2_std\"]\n          dominant_dofs: [1, 2, 3]\n          interpretation: \"Robot-state offset manifests as position bias; CRM focuses on position DOF conformal radii\"\n        light:\n          high_gradient_features: [\"ellipsoid_volume\", \"dof3_std\", \"dof5_std\", \"dof6_std\"]\n          dominant_dofs: [3, 5, 6]\n          interpretation: \"Lighting affects depth (z) and wrist rotation perception\"\n        gripper_gradient_ratio_vs_position: 0.31\n      top5_features_overall: [\"dof1_std\", \"dof2_std\", \"ellipsoid_volume\", \"dof3_conformal_r\", \"dof1_conformal_r\"]\n    result_rationale: >\n      CRM learns perturbation-specific attention patterns because different perturbation\n      types produce distinctive uncertainty signatures in the 45D feature space. Camera\n      perturbation inflates position DOF (1-3) uncertainties while leaving rotation/gripper\n      relatively stable — CRM correctly attends to position features. Noise uniformly\n      inflates all DOFs — CRM relies on the global ellipsoid volume as a summary statistic\n      plus distributed per-DOF features. Robot-state offset creates systematic position\n      bias detectable via conformal radii (which capture directional shift, not just\n      variance). The gripper DOF consistently receives low gradient magnitude (0.31× of\n      position DOFs) because gripper actions are discrete-like (open/close) and robust to\n      most perturbations. This perturbation-specific feature attention is the mechanistic\n      evidence for \"dimension-selective correction\" — CRM doesn't apply uniform corrections\n      but routes different perturbation types through different feature pathways.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 7: Top-5 Features by Gradient Magnitude per Perturbation\"\n        columns: [\"Perturbation\", \"Rank 1\", \"Rank 2\", \"Rank 3\", \"Rank 4\", \"Rank 5\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Camera\", \"dof1_std\", \"dof2_std\", \"dof3_std\", \"dof1_cr\", \"dof2_cr\"]\n          - [\"Noise\", \"ellipsoid_vol\", \"dof1_std\", \"dof4_std\", \"dof5_std\", \"dof6_std\"]\n          - [\"Robot-state\", \"dof1_cr\", \"dof2_cr\", \"dof3_cr\", \"dof1_std\", \"dof2_std\"]\n          - [\"Light\", \"ellipsoid_vol\", \"dof3_std\", \"dof5_std\", \"dof6_std\", \"dof3_cr\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 9: 45×7 heatmap of normalized gradient magnitudes (features × perturbation types). Clear block-diagonal structure visible: position DOFs dominate for camera/robot-state, rotation DOFs for light, and distributed pattern for noise. This is the key mechanistic figure.\"\n      - \"Fig 9 inset: Gradient magnitude ratio (position DOFs / rotation DOFs) across perturbation types, showing >3× ratio for camera/robot-state and ~1× for noise.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The feature importance heatmap is arguably the most compelling figure in the paper.\n      The block-diagonal structure provides direct mechanistic evidence that CRM performs\n      dimension-selective correction — not as a design constraint but as an emergent behavior\n      from training on per-DOF conformal features. This figure should appear prominently\n      (main paper, not supplementary). It directly supports the \"first method to use\n      structured multivariate CP statistics as learned correction features\" positioning\n      by showing that the structure in the features is actually being exploited.\n\n  - name: \"SnapFlow Integration (K=10 × 1-step)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_013\"\n    description: >\n      Replace 100-step flow-matching sampling with 1-step SnapFlow distilled generation.\n      K=10 samples generated via 1-step denoising. Test (a) sample diversity preservation\n      (measured by pairwise distance distribution compared to 100-step), (b) CGS feature\n      quality (covariance condition number), (c) downstream ACR-VLA performance, (d) wall-\n      clock inference time.\n    expected_results:\n      snapflow_sr_liberoplus: 72.5\n      snapflow_vs_full_gap_pp: 1.6\n      inference_time_snapflow_ms: 45\n      inference_time_full_ms: 385\n      speedup_factor: 8.6\n      sample_diversity_preservation_pct: 82\n      covariance_condition_number_snapflow: 22.3\n      covariance_condition_number_full: 14.7\n    result_rationale: >\n      1-step distilled generation preserves ~82% of sample diversity because SnapFlow's\n      consistency distillation maintains the marginal distribution but compresses the\n      sampling trajectory, losing some of the fine-grained stochasticity that 100-step\n      ODE integration explores. This manifests as slightly higher covariance condition\n      numbers (22.3 vs 14.7) — the covariance is still estimable but less well-conditioned\n      because sample diversity is reduced. The 1.6pp performance gap is modest because\n      Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage compensates for the slightly less stable covariance estimation,\n      and CRM is somewhat robust to covariance estimation noise (it learned smooth mappings\n      from noisy features during training with only K=10 samples). The 8.6× speedup (385ms\n      → 45ms) brings ACR-VLA inference into real-time territory for 10Hz control, making\n      it practically deployable. The speedup is slightly less than 100× (the sampling\n      step reduction) because CGS computation and CRM forward pass (together ~35ms)\n      dominate when sampling is fast.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 8: SnapFlow Speed-Accuracy Tradeoff\"\n        columns: [\"Variant\", \"Sampling Steps\", \"Inference (ms)\", \"L-Plus SR%\", \"Δ vs Full\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Full (100-step)\", 100, 385, 74.1, \"—\"]\n          - [\"SnapFlow (1-step)\", 1, 45, 72.5, \"-1.6\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Supplementary Fig: (Left) Pairwise sample distance distributions for 100-step vs 1-step, showing compressed but preserved distribution shape. (Right) Pareto frontier of inference time vs success rate, showing SnapFlow is near-optimal.\"\n    analysis: >\n      SnapFlow integration demonstrates practical viability: 1.6pp cost for 8.6× speedup\n      is an excellent tradeoff for real-time applications. The 82% diversity preservation\n      confirms that 1-step distilled sampling captures the bulk of epistemic uncertainty\n      even though it loses some of the tail diversity from full ODE integration. This\n      result should be presented as a deployment-oriented contribution rather than a core\n      scientific finding — it shows ACR-VLA's inference overhead can be made negligible\n      with off-the-shelf distillation.\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11112,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11112},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3a8fe5df-a524-42fe-ad30-68007c28aa51","uuid":"6737006a-cf09-4a4f-a5a8-a7bf8bd2b9c8"}
{"type":"result","subtype":"success","is_error":false,"api_error_status":null,"duration_ms":310276,"duration_api_ms":312006,"num_turns":1,"result":"```yaml\ntier: best\nnarrative_guidance: >\n  Original framing holds intact. Headline: \"ACR-VLA achieves dimension-selective action\n  correction via per-DOF conformal features, improving base VLA by 11.8% on LIBERO-Plus\n  and pushing SRPO SOTA from 82.1% to 86.4%.\" All three claims supported with clear\n  ablation evidence. Per-perturbation breakdown shows largest gains on camera/noise/robot-state\n  (coverable by synthetic augmentation), honest limitation on layout/language. Feature\n  importance analysis shows CRM attends to different DOF uncertainty dimensions under\n  different perturbation types — strong mechanistic evidence for dimension-selective\n  correction. Position as first method to use structured multivariate CP statistics\n  as learned correction features.\n\nimagined_experiments:\n  - name: \"ACR-VLA Full Pipeline (SmolVLA base)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_001\"\n    description: >\n      End-to-end ACR-VLA: CGS (K=10, Ledoit-Wolf) extracts 45D per-DOF conformal features\n      from flow-matching samples, ACI+COC adapts conformal threshold online, CRM (131K params)\n      outputs dimension-selective action correction. CRM trained on LIBERO demos + 5 synthetic\n      augmentation types (camera jitter, Gaussian noise, robot-state offset, lighting shift,\n      background swap). Evaluated on LIBERO (10 tasks × 20 rollouts) and LIBERO-Plus L3–L5\n      (7 perturbation types × 5 tasks × 20 rollouts).\n    expected_results:\n      libero_nominal_sr_base: 97.2\n      libero_nominal_sr_acrvla: 96.8\n      libero_nominal_degradation_pp: 0.4\n      liberoplus_l3l5_sr_base: 62.3\n      liberoplus_l3l5_sr_acrvla: 74.1\n      delta_over_base_liberoplus_sr_pp: 11.8\n      crm_inference_overhead_ms: 3.2\n      total_inference_time_ms: 385\n      cgs_covariance_condition_number_median: 14.7\n      cgs_ledoit_shrinkage_intensity_mean: 0.38\n    result_rationale: >\n      K=10 Ledoit-Wolf covariance at p/n=0.7 retains sufficient cross-DOF structure because\n      shrinkage intensity ~0.38 strikes the bias-variance sweet spot — enough regularization\n      to prevent rank deficiency while preserving the dominant eigenvectors that encode\n      meaningful DOF covariance (validated in MVP where K=10 degraded only ~15pp vs oracle).\n      Flow-matching samples at K=10 capture meaningful epistemic uncertainty because the\n      learned ODE solver's stochasticity at different initial conditions spans the model's\n      posterior predictive — under distribution shift (camera/noise/robot-state perturbations\n      covered by synthetic augmentation), sample spread along affected DOFs increases\n      monotonically with perturbation severity. CRM's 131K-param capacity is sufficient\n      because the task is low-dimensional regression (45D→7D residual) with strong inductive\n      bias from the conformal feature structure; overfitting is controlled by the feature\n      bottleneck rather than requiring larger capacity. The 11.8pp gain lands in the upper\n      half of the [8,15] range because synthetic augmentation covers 5 of 7 perturbation\n      types well, and the two uncovered types (layout/language) have smaller base failure\n      rates, limiting the dilution of average gains. Nominal degradation is minimal (0.4pp)\n      because CRM learns near-zero residuals when conformal features indicate low uncertainty\n      (in-distribution), effectively self-gating.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 1: Main Results on LIBERO and LIBERO-Plus\"\n        columns: [\"Method\", \"LIBERO (nominal) SR%\", \"LIBERO-Plus L3 SR%\", \"LIBERO-Plus L4 SR%\", \"LIBERO-Plus L5 SR%\", \"L3-L5 Avg SR%\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"SmolVLA (base)\", 97.2, 71.8, 60.4, 54.7, 62.3]\n          - [\"SmolVLA + ReconVLA\", 96.5, 75.2, 64.8, 59.1, 66.4]\n          - [\"SmolVLA + ACR-VLA (ours)\", 96.8, 82.6, 72.3, 67.4, 74.1]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 2: Success rate vs perturbation level (L1–L5) curves for base SmolVLA, ReconVLA, and ACR-VLA. ACR-VLA curve degrades more gracefully, maintaining >65% at L5 where base drops below 55%.\"\n      - \"Fig 3: Example rollout visualization comparing base VLA (fails at grasp due to noisy wrist rotation) vs ACR-VLA (CRM corrects wrist DOFs 5-6 while preserving position DOFs 1-3), with overlaid conformal ellipsoid cross-sections.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The 11.8pp improvement validates the core thesis that structured per-DOF conformal\n      features enable meaningful action correction. The near-zero nominal degradation (0.4pp)\n      confirms CRM learns to be inactive when uncertainty is low. Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage\n      intensity averaging 0.38 indicates moderate regularization — the covariance is neither\n      collapsed to diagonal (which would lose cross-DOF information) nor unregularized\n      (which would be unstable at p/n=0.7). Condition numbers in the teens indicate\n      well-conditioned estimation. Per-level breakdown shows gains increase with perturbation\n      severity (L3: +10.8, L4: +11.9, L5: +12.7), consistent with epistemic uncertainty\n      being more informative under stronger shifts.\n\n  - name: \"Ablation A3 — CRM without Conformal Features\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_002\"\n    description: >\n      CRM receives only the current observation embedding and base action as input (no 45D\n      conformal features). Architecture identical to full CRM but input dimension reduced\n      from 45+obs_dim to obs_dim. This is equivalent to an A2C2-style generic observation-\n      conditioned residual policy. Same training data and procedure as plan_001.\n    expected_results:\n      liberoplus_l3l5_sr_a3: 66.8\n      a3_gap_full_vs_no_conformal_pp: 7.3\n      libero_nominal_sr_a3: 96.4\n    result_rationale: >\n      Without conformal features, CRM can only learn a generic observation-conditioned\n      residual — it knows something is wrong (via visual mismatch in the observation) but\n      not which action dimensions are unreliable or how they co-vary. This forces it to\n      apply uniform correction across all DOFs, which helps on average but sometimes\n      degrades already-correct dimensions. The 7.3pp gap (well within the [5,10] make-or-\n      break range) arises because conformal features provide two critical pieces of information\n      the observation alone cannot: (1) per-DOF uncertainty magnitude, allowing selective\n      correction intensity, and (2) cross-DOF covariance structure, allowing coordinated\n      multi-joint corrections. The A3 variant still outperforms the no-correction base by\n      4.5pp because the observation embedding carries some perturbation-relevant signal, but\n      this improvement is substantially less efficient than the conformal-conditioned version.\n      Nominal performance degrades slightly more (0.8pp vs 0.4pp) because without uncertainty\n      gating, CRM occasionally applies spurious corrections in-distribution.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 2 (row): Ablation A3 in main ablation table\"\n        columns: [\"Variant\", \"Conformal Features\", \"CRM\", \"ACI\", \"LIBERO SR%\", \"L-Plus L3-L5 SR%\", \"Δ vs Full\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"A3: No conformal\", \"✗\", \"✓\", \"✓\", 96.4, 66.8, \"-7.3\"]\n          - [\"Full ACR-VLA\", \"✓\", \"✓\", \"✓\", 96.8, 74.1, \"—\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Ablation bar chart (Fig 5a): side-by-side bars for Full vs A3 across L3/L4/L5, showing the gap widens at higher perturbation levels (L3: -5.8, L4: -7.6, L5: -8.5).\"\n    analysis: >\n      The 7.3pp gap is the strongest evidence for Claim 1. It demonstrates that conformal\n      features are not merely redundant with observation information — they provide a\n      complementary uncertainty signal that the observation encoder alone cannot extract.\n      The widening gap at higher perturbation levels (L5 gap: 8.5pp) confirms that conformal\n      features become increasingly valuable as distribution shift intensifies, consistent\n      with epistemic uncertainty scaling with shift magnitude. This ablation cleanly\n      separates \"knowing what to correct\" (conformal features) from \"being able to correct\"\n      (CRM capacity), showing both are necessary.\n\n  - name: \"Ablation A4 — Scalar Uncertainty (ReconVLA-style)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_003\"\n    description: >\n      CRM receives scalar uncertainty features instead of 45D per-DOF features. Scalar\n      features: mean Euclidean pairwise distance among K=10 samples (1D), mean sample std\n      (1D), maximum sample std (1D) — total 3D uncertainty input, matching ReconVLA's\n      information granularity. Same CRM architecture scaled for 3D uncertainty input.\n    expected_results:\n      liberoplus_l3l5_sr_a4: 69.7\n      a4_gap_perDOF_vs_scalar_pp: 4.4\n      libero_nominal_sr_a4: 96.6\n    result_rationale: >\n      Scalar uncertainty tells CRM \"how uncertain the model is overall\" but not \"which DOFs\n      are uncertain.\" This forces CRM to learn an average correction profile rather than\n      dimension-selective adjustment. The 4.4pp gap (within [3,6]) reflects the information\n      loss from collapsing 7-DOF covariance structure into scalars. Specifically, scalar\n      features cannot distinguish between (a) high uncertainty in wrist rotation only (correct\n      fix: adjust DOFs 5-6-7) and (b) high uncertainty in position only (correct fix: adjust\n      DOFs 1-2-3) — both map to the same scalar value. The scalar variant still outperforms\n      A3 (no conformal) by 2.9pp because even aggregate uncertainty magnitude is useful for\n      scaling correction intensity. The gap is moderate rather than large because many\n      perturbation types affect multiple DOFs simultaneously, partially reducing the\n      advantage of per-DOF resolution. The per-DOF advantage is expected to be most\n      pronounced on perturbations that selectively affect specific DOF subsets (e.g.,\n      camera perturbation primarily affects position inference while sparing gripper).\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 2 (row): Ablation A4\"\n        columns: [\"Variant\", \"Uncertainty Dim\", \"LIBERO SR%\", \"L-Plus L3-L5 SR%\", \"Δ vs Full\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"A4: Scalar (3D)\", \"3\", 96.6, 69.7, \"-4.4\"]\n          - [\"Full ACR-VLA (45D)\", \"45\", 96.8, 74.1, \"—\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 5b: Per-perturbation-type gap between Full and A4. Largest gap on camera (+6.2pp) and robot-state (+5.8pp) perturbations where DOF-selectivity matters most; smallest on noise (+2.1pp) which affects all DOFs uniformly.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The scalar-to-perDOF upgrade yields 4.4pp, confirming that structured uncertainty is\n      strictly more informative than aggregate uncertainty. Combined with A3 (7.3pp gap for\n      any-uncertainty-vs-none), we see a clear hierarchy: per-DOF (74.1) > scalar (69.7) >\n      none (66.8) > base (62.3). This ordering is the central empirical result supporting\n      Claim 1 and validates the core motivation for multivariate conformal prediction over\n      scalar approaches.\n\n  - name: \"SRPO Stacking\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_004\"\n    description: >\n      ACR-VLA applied on top of SRPO-finetuned SmolVLA. New CRM trained on SRPO checkpoint\n      using same procedure (LIBERO demos + synthetic augmentation). CGS calibration set\n      re-computed from SRPO checkpoint's flow-matching samples. Evaluated on LIBERO-Plus\n      L3-L5.\n    expected_results:\n      srpo_base_sr: 82.1\n      srpo_plus_acrvla_sr: 86.4\n      srpo_stacking_gain_pp: 4.3\n      libero_nominal_srpo_acrvla: 97.8\n      libero_nominal_srpo_base: 98.1\n      nominal_degradation_pp: 0.3\n    result_rationale: >\n      SRPO improves the base policy's training-time robustness by optimizing for perturbation\n      invariance, but residual errors remain — particularly on out-of-distribution perturbation\n      combinations and severity levels beyond training coverage. ACR-VLA operates at inference\n      time, exploiting the complementary signal that SRPO's flow-matching samples still\n      exhibit elevated epistemic uncertainty on these residual failure modes. The 4.3pp gain\n      (within [3,6]) is smaller than the 11.8pp gain on vanilla SmolVLA because SRPO has\n      already addressed the \"easy\" failures (perturbations well-covered by training-time\n      augmentation), leaving harder cases where CRM correction is less reliable. The stacked\n      86.4% (within [85,88]) represents new SOTA on LIBERO-Plus. The gain is non-trivial\n      because ACR-VLA and SRPO address complementary failure modes: SRPO reduces the frequency\n      of high-uncertainty predictions (training-time), while ACR-VLA corrects the remaining\n      high-uncertainty predictions (inference-time). Nominal degradation is minimal because\n      SRPO's already-robust in-distribution performance leaves little room for CRM to\n      interfere.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 3: Stacking Results\"\n        columns: [\"Method\", \"Training-time\", \"Inference-time\", \"LIBERO SR%\", \"L-Plus L3-L5 SR%\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"SmolVLA\", \"—\", \"—\", 97.2, 62.3]\n          - [\"SmolVLA + ACR-VLA\", \"—\", \"ACR-VLA\", 96.8, 74.1]\n          - [\"SRPO\", \"SRPO\", \"—\", 98.1, 82.1]\n          - [\"SRPO + ACR-VLA\", \"SRPO\", \"ACR-VLA\", 97.8, 86.4]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 6: 2×2 matrix plot showing training-time (SRPO vs none) × inference-time (ACR-VLA vs none). Both axes contribute, and gains are roughly additive (11.8 + 4.3 ≈ expected if orthogonal), confirming complementarity.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The 4.3pp stacking gain is the headline number for Claim 3: inference-time conformal\n      correction provides value orthogonal to training-time optimization. The approximate\n      additivity (base→ACR: +11.8, SRPO→SRPO+ACR: +4.3, sum 16.1 vs actual 24.1 total\n      improvement from base to SRPO+ACR) suggests partial but substantial complementarity.\n      The new SOTA of 86.4% pushes LIBERO-Plus performance into a regime where the\n      remaining failures are dominated by layout/language perturbations that neither\n      synthetic augmentation nor training-time optimization can fully address.\n\n  - name: \"COC Signal Validation\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_005\"\n    description: >\n      Three analyses: (a) Spearman rank correlation between per-episode COC coverage\n      fraction (fraction of timesteps where chunk-overlap conformal prediction set contains\n      the next observed action) and binary task success. (b) ACI convergence plot: running\n      conformal threshold α_t over episodes. (c) Ellipsoid volume comparison: ACI-adapted\n      vs static calibration at matched marginal coverage (90%).\n    expected_results:\n      coc_success_spearman_rho: 0.62\n      coc_success_spearman_p_value: 0.00003\n      aci_convergence_episodes: 45\n      aci_final_alpha_std_across_tasks: 0.018\n      aci_ellipsoid_volume_narrowing_pct: 31\n      aci_marginal_coverage_achieved: 0.904\n      static_marginal_coverage_achieved: 0.897\n      aci_mean_ellipsoid_volume: 0.043\n      static_mean_ellipsoid_volume: 0.062\n    result_rationale: >\n      COC achieves ρ=0.62 (within [0.5,0.7]) because chunk overlap is a reliable proxy for\n      prediction quality: when the model's predictions at overlapping chunk positions are\n      self-consistent, the action trajectory is likely coherent and on-track; inconsistency\n      signals the model is uncertain about the trajectory, which correlates with failure.\n      The correlation is strong but not perfect because (a) some failures are due to\n      execution errors in correct predictions, and (b) some successes occur despite moderate\n      COC violations (the robot recovers from brief uncertainty). ACI converges in ~45\n      episodes because the step-level COC signal provides dense feedback (~200 updates per\n      episode × 45 episodes ≈ 9000 gradient steps on α), much faster than episode-level\n      binary signal. The 31% ellipsoid volume narrowing (within [20,40]) occurs because ACI\n      tightens the threshold under in-distribution conditions (where static calibration is\n      conservative) while loosening it under shift (where static calibration is too tight),\n      resulting in better-calibrated, tighter prediction sets on average. This is the\n      standard ACI advantage: adaptive thresholds converge to the optimal per-context\n      threshold rather than using a single global threshold.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 4: COC Validation Metrics\"\n        columns: [\"Metric\", \"Value\", \"95% CI\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"COC-Success Spearman ρ\", 0.62, \"[0.51, 0.72]\"]\n          - [\"ACI convergence (episodes to |α_t - α*| < 0.01)\", 45, \"[38, 55]\"]\n          - [\"Ellipsoid volume (ACI)\", 0.043, \"[0.038, 0.049]\"]\n          - [\"Ellipsoid volume (static)\", 0.062, \"[0.055, 0.070]\"]\n          - [\"Volume narrowing\", \"31%\", \"[22%, 38%]\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 4a: Scatter plot of per-episode COC coverage fraction vs task success (binary), with logistic regression fit and Spearman ρ annotation. Clear separation between success/failure clusters with COC coverage threshold ~0.75.\"\n      - \"Fig 4b: ACI convergence plot — running α_t over 200 episodes, showing convergence to stable value by episode ~45. Shaded region shows ±1 std across 5 seeds.\"\n      - \"Fig 4c: Box plot comparing ellipsoid volumes under ACI vs static calibration at matched 90% coverage. ACI distribution is tighter and shifted left (smaller volumes).\"\n    analysis: >\n      ρ=0.62 validates COC as a self-referential progress signal — no external supervision\n      needed. The convergence speed (~45 episodes) is practical for online deployment.\n      The 31% volume narrowing demonstrates ACI's value: at the same coverage guarantee,\n      ACI produces tighter conformal sets, which in turn give CRM more precise uncertainty\n      features. This validates Claim 2's core proposition that self-referential ACI\n      maintains calibrated uncertainty without external supervision.\n\n  - name: \"Ablation A1 — No ACI (Static Calibration)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_006\"\n    description: >\n      ACR-VLA with static conformal threshold (calibrated once on calibration set, never\n      updated). CGS and CRM identical to full pipeline. Isolates ACI online adaptation\n      contribution.\n    expected_results:\n      liberoplus_l3l5_sr_a1: 71.2\n      a1_gap_vs_full_pp: 2.9\n      libero_nominal_sr_a1: 96.9\n    result_rationale: >\n      Without ACI, the conformal threshold is fixed at calibration-set level. Under\n      distribution shift, this static threshold is miscalibrated — typically too loose\n      (calibrated on in-distribution data where uncertainty is lower), causing conformal\n      sets to be too large and conformal features to overestimate uncertainty. CRM trained\n      on well-calibrated features during training then receives inflated uncertainty signals\n      at test time, leading to over-correction. The 2.9pp gap is moderate because CRM has\n      some robustness to feature distribution shift (it learned a smooth mapping), but the\n      systematic bias in uncertainty overestimation degrades precision. The gap is not\n      catastrophic because CGS's structural information (which DOFs are uncertain, cross-DOF\n      covariance) is still informative even with a miscalibrated scale. ACI's primary\n      contribution is recalibrating the scale, not changing the structure.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 2 (row): Ablation A1\"\n        columns: [\"Variant\", \"ACI\", \"LIBERO SR%\", \"L-Plus SR%\", \"Δ vs Full\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"A1: Static calibration\", \"✗\", 96.9, 71.2, \"-2.9\"]\n    figures_description: []\n    analysis: >\n      The 2.9pp ACI contribution is meaningful but secondary to the conformal features\n      themselves (7.3pp A3 gap) and per-DOF structure (4.4pp A4 gap). This ordering\n      (features > granularity > adaptation) supports the paper's emphasis on per-DOF\n      conformal features as the primary contribution, with ACI as a valuable but not\n      essential enhancement.\n\n  - name: \"Ablation A2 — ACI without CRM (Sample Selection Only)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_007\"\n    description: >\n      CGS + COC + ACI used for multivariate-CP-guided sample selection (select the sample\n      closest to the conformal set centroid), but no learned CRM correction. Extends\n      ReconVLA's selection paradigm to multivariate CP. Isolates CRM contribution.\n    expected_results:\n      liberoplus_l3l5_sr_a2: 66.5\n      a2_gap_vs_full_pp: 7.6\n      libero_nominal_sr_a2: 97.0\n    result_rationale: >\n      Sample selection alone (even with multivariate CP guidance) is fundamentally limited\n      because it can only choose among the K=10 existing samples — it cannot synthesize an\n      action better than any individual sample. When all K samples are biased in the same\n      direction (common under distribution shift), selection cannot correct the bias. CRM's\n      learned residual can extrapolate beyond the sample set. The 7.6pp gap quantifies the\n      value of learned correction over pure selection. The A2 variant still improves over\n      base by 4.2pp because multivariate CP-guided selection outperforms random/mean\n      aggregation: it identifies which sample is most consistent with the conformal set\n      geometry, effectively performing soft outlier rejection. This improvement is comparable\n      to ReconVLA's reported gains, validating the multivariate extension. Nominal\n      performance is slightly better than full ACR-VLA (97.0 vs 96.8) because sample\n      selection is more conservative than learned correction in-distribution.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 2 (row): Ablation A2\"\n        columns: [\"Variant\", \"CRM\", \"LIBERO SR%\", \"L-Plus SR%\", \"Δ vs Full\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"A2: Selection only\", \"✗\", 97.0, 66.5, \"-7.6\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Comparison bar in Fig 5a showing A2 (selection) vs A3 (no conformal) at similar levels (~66-67%), demonstrating that without CRM, even structured selection provides limited advantage over observation-conditioned residual.\"\n    analysis: >\n      A2 and A3 perform similarly (~66.5 vs 66.8%), revealing an interesting symmetry:\n      knowing what's uncertain without being able to correct it (A2) is roughly as valuable\n      as being able to correct without knowing what's uncertain (A3). Full ACR-VLA's 7+pp\n      advantage over both confirms the synergy is super-additive — CRM needs conformal\n      features to know where to correct, and conformal features need CRM to act on the\n      information.\n\n  - name: \"Per-Perturbation Breakdown\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_008\"\n    description: >\n      ACR-VLA gain decomposed across all 7 LIBERO-Plus perturbation types at L4 severity.\n      20 rollouts × 5 tasks per perturbation type. Reports both base and ACR-VLA SR per\n      perturbation.\n    expected_results:\n      perturbation_gains:\n        camera:\n          base_sr: 52.0\n          acrvla_sr: 67.2\n          gain_pp: 15.2\n        noise:\n          base_sr: 46.0\n          acrvla_sr: 63.8\n          gain_pp: 17.8\n        robot_state:\n          base_sr: 55.0\n          acrvla_sr: 69.1\n          gain_pp: 14.1\n        light:\n          base_sr: 62.0\n          acrvla_sr: 73.3\n          gain_pp: 11.3\n        background:\n          base_sr: 66.0\n          acrvla_sr: 75.4\n          gain_pp: 9.4\n        layout:\n          base_sr: 72.0\n          acrvla_sr: 75.2\n          gain_pp: 3.2\n        language:\n          base_sr: 74.0\n          acrvla_sr: 75.8\n          gain_pp: 1.8\n      crm_per_perturbation_gain_camera_noise_robotstate_pp_avg: 15.7\n    result_rationale: >\n      Camera/noise/robot-state show the largest gains (14-18pp, within [10,20]) because\n      these perturbation types are well-covered by synthetic augmentation during CRM\n      training AND produce distinctive per-DOF uncertainty signatures that CRM can exploit.\n      Camera perturbation primarily inflates uncertainty in end-effector position DOFs\n      (DOFs 1-3) while leaving rotation/gripper relatively unaffected — CRM learns to\n      selectively correct position. Noise affects all DOFs but with characteristic\n      covariance patterns that CRM recognizes from training. Robot-state offsets produce\n      systematic biases in specific DOFs that CRM compensates via learned residuals.\n      Light/background show moderate gains (9-11pp) because they affect visual features\n      globally, producing less DOF-selective uncertainty patterns — CRM still helps but\n      the correction is more diffuse. Layout and language show minimal gains (2-3pp)\n      because (a) layout changes are not covered by synthetic augmentation, and (b)\n      language perturbations affect task semantics rather than action execution, producing\n      uncertainty patterns CRM never saw during training. This honest limitation is\n      intrinsic: no inference-time action correction can fix misunderstood instructions.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 5: Per-Perturbation Breakdown (L4)\"\n        columns: [\"Perturbation\", \"Base SR%\", \"ACR-VLA SR%\", \"Gain (pp)\", \"Covered by Synth Aug?\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Camera\", 52.0, 67.2, \"+15.2\", \"✓\"]\n          - [\"Noise\", 46.0, 63.8, \"+17.8\", \"✓\"]\n          - [\"Robot-state\", 55.0, 69.1, \"+14.1\", \"✓\"]\n          - [\"Light\", 62.0, 73.3, \"+11.3\", \"✓\"]\n          - [\"Background\", 66.0, 75.4, \"+9.4\", \"✓\"]\n          - [\"Layout\", 72.0, 75.2, \"+3.2\", \"✗\"]\n          - [\"Language\", 74.0, 75.8, \"+1.8\", \"✗\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 7a: Grouped bar chart (base vs ACR-VLA) across 7 perturbation types, sorted by gain. Clear visual separation between augmentation-covered (left, large gains) and uncovered (right, small gains) perturbation types.\"\n      - \"Fig 7b: Gain magnitude vs synthetic augmentation coverage annotation, supporting the honest limitation narrative.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The per-perturbation breakdown provides the most nuanced evidence for the paper's\n      narrative. The strong correlation between synthetic augmentation coverage and gain\n      magnitude (ρ≈0.9 across 7 types) is both a strength (mechanistic explanation) and\n      an honest limitation (layout/language resistance). Average gain on covered types\n      (camera/noise/robot-state) is 15.7pp, well within the [10,20] best-tier range.\n      This breakdown should be prominently featured to preempt reviewer concerns about\n      cherry-picked aggregate numbers.\n\n  - name: \"Ablation A5 — CGS vs Bonferroni\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_009\"\n    description: >\n      Replace joint Mahalanobis-based CGS (which uses full covariance) with per-DOF\n      Bonferroni-corrected marginal conformal prediction (treats each DOF independently,\n      applies α/7 per DOF). Same CRM architecture, 45D features but covariance-derived\n      features (off-diagonal ellipsoid axes) are uninformative.\n    expected_results:\n      liberoplus_l3l5_sr_bonferroni: 72.0\n      cgs_vs_bonferroni_gap_pp: 2.1\n    result_rationale: >\n      Bonferroni correction treats DOFs independently, discarding cross-DOF covariance\n      structure. This loses two types of information: (1) which DOF pairs co-vary under\n      uncertainty (e.g., shoulder pitch and elbow pitch are coupled), and (2) the ellipsoid\n      orientation that indicates the principal axes of uncertainty. The 2.1pp gap is modest\n      because per-DOF marginal features (std, marginal conformal radius) still capture most\n      of the variance — cross-DOF structure provides incremental but real value. Bonferroni\n      also produces overly conservative individual intervals (α/7 is much smaller than\n      needed when DOFs are correlated), inflating conformal radii and reducing CRM's ability\n      to discriminate truly uncertain from merely correlated DOFs.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 2 (row): Ablation A5\"\n        columns: [\"Variant\", \"CP Method\", \"L-Plus SR%\", \"Δ vs Full\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"A5: Bonferroni\", \"Marginal + correction\", 72.0, \"-2.1\"]\n          - [\"Full: CGS\", \"Joint Mahalanobis\", 74.1, \"—\"]\n    figures_description: []\n    analysis: >\n      The 2.1pp CGS advantage validates the use of multivariate CP over marginal Bonferroni\n      but is not a massive effect. This is expected — most of the per-DOF advantage comes\n      from having per-DOF resolution at all (4.4pp A4 gap), not from the specific covariance\n      structure. Reviewers may probe this; the response is that CGS provides a theoretically\n      principled joint guarantee while Bonferroni is known to be conservative, and the\n      2.1pp gap represents a free lunch from using the correct statistical framework.\n\n  - name: \"Ablation A6 — COC vs Episode-Level ACI\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_010\"\n    description: >\n      Replace COC per-step signal with episode-level binary task success for ACI updates.\n      ACI threshold updates once per episode (success → tighten, failure → loosen) instead\n      of per-step via COC. Same CRM and CGS.\n    expected_results:\n      liberoplus_l3l5_sr_episode_aci: 72.6\n      coc_vs_episode_gap_pp: 1.5\n      aci_convergence_episodes_episode_level: 120\n    result_rationale: >\n      Episode-level binary signal is sparser (~1 update per 200 steps vs ~200 updates per\n      episode with COC) and noisier (success/failure is binary, COC coverage fraction is\n      continuous). This slows ACI convergence from ~45 to ~120 episodes and produces a\n      coarser threshold estimate. The 1.5pp gap is modest because both variants eventually\n      converge to similar thresholds — the difference is convergence speed and precision.\n      COC's advantage is primarily practical (faster adaptation in non-stationary settings)\n      rather than asymptotic. In a fixed-distribution evaluation, the gap would shrink\n      further given enough episodes.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 2 (row): Ablation A6\"\n        columns: [\"Variant\", \"ACI Signal\", \"Convergence (eps)\", \"L-Plus SR%\", \"Δ vs Full\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"A6: Episode-level ACI\", \"Binary success\", 120, 72.6, \"-1.5\"]\n          - [\"Full: COC ACI\", \"Per-step COC\", 45, 74.1, \"—\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Supplementary: ACI convergence curves for COC vs episode-level, showing 2.7× faster convergence with COC.\"\n    analysis: >\n      COC's per-step signal provides faster and more precise ACI convergence. The 1.5pp\n      practical advantage justifies COC's inclusion, though the paper should honestly note\n      that the asymptotic difference is smaller. The convergence speed advantage (45 vs 120\n      episodes) is the stronger argument — in non-stationary deployments where perturbation\n      types change over time, fast adaptation is critical.\n\n  - name: \"Ablation A7 — DiU Aleatoric vs CGS Epistemic\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_011\"\n    description: >\n      Three variants: (a) CRM with DiU single-pass aleatoric uncertainty features (7D per-DOF\n      aleatoric std), (b) CRM with CGS epistemic features (45D, full ACR-VLA), (c) CRM with\n      combined epistemic+aleatoric features (52D). Validates that epistemic uncertainty is\n      the correct signal for distribution shift correction.\n    expected_results:\n      liberoplus_l3l5_sr_diu_aleatoric: 67.4\n      liberoplus_l3l5_sr_cgs_epistemic: 74.1\n      liberoplus_l3l5_sr_combined_52d: 74.8\n      epistemic_vs_aleatoric_gap_pp: 6.7\n      combined_vs_epistemic_marginal_gain_pp: 0.7\n      libero_nominal_sr_diu: 95.8\n      libero_nominal_sr_combined: 96.6\n    result_rationale: >\n      DiU's aleatoric uncertainty captures inherent action stochasticity — it's high even\n      in-distribution (at decision boundaries, near contact transitions). This makes it a\n      poor discriminator for distribution shift: the model reports high aleatoric uncertainty\n      regardless of whether it's in-distribution or shifted. CRM trained on aleatoric\n      features over-corrects in-distribution (nominal degradation 1.4pp) and under-corrects\n      under shift (cannot distinguish shift-induced from inherent uncertainty). CGS epistemic\n      uncertainty, by contrast, is low in-distribution and elevates specifically under shift,\n      providing the causal signal CRM needs. The 6.7pp gap directly validates the paper's\n      theoretical motivation: epistemic uncertainty (what the model doesn't know) is the\n      correct conditioning signal for correction, not aleatoric uncertainty (inherent\n      stochasticity). The combined 52D variant gains only 0.7pp over epistemic alone,\n      confirming that aleatoric features are largely redundant given epistemic features —\n      the marginal information in aleatoric variance (contact transition timing) provides\n      minimal additional value for correction. This ablation provides the clearest\n      mechanistic evidence for the CGS design choice.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 6: Epistemic vs Aleatoric Uncertainty\"\n        columns: [\"Uncertainty Type\", \"Dim\", \"LIBERO SR%\", \"L-Plus SR%\", \"Δ vs CGS\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"DiU aleatoric\", 7, 95.8, 67.4, \"-6.7\"]\n          - [\"CGS epistemic\", 45, 96.8, 74.1, \"—\"]\n          - [\"Combined\", 52, 96.6, 74.8, \"+0.7\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 8: Uncertainty magnitude distributions (violin plots) for epistemic vs aleatoric features under in-distribution vs shifted conditions. Epistemic shows clear separation (low in-dist, high shifted); aleatoric shows high variance in both conditions with substantial overlap.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The epistemic-vs-aleatoric distinction is a clean, interpretable result that reviewers\n      will appreciate. The 6.7pp gap is large enough to be unambiguous, and the combined\n      variant's marginal 0.7pp gain confirms the theoretical prediction that aleatoric\n      information is orthogonal but not additive to epistemic features for correction\n      purposes. Recommend including the violin plot (Fig 8) as it provides the most\n      intuitive mechanistic explanation.\n\n  - name: \"Feature Importance Analysis\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_012\"\n    description: >\n      Compute CRM gradient magnitude w.r.t. each of the 45 uncertainty input features,\n      averaged over test episodes, broken down by perturbation type. Visualize as 45×7\n      heatmap (features × perturbation types). Validates dimension-selective correction\n      mechanism.\n    expected_results:\n      feature_importance_patterns:\n        camera:\n          high_gradient_features: [\"dof1_std\", \"dof2_std\", \"dof3_std\", \"dof1_conformal_r\", \"dof2_conformal_r\"]\n          dominant_dofs: [1, 2, 3]\n          interpretation: \"Camera perturbation primarily affects position inference; CRM attends to position DOF uncertainties\"\n        noise:\n          high_gradient_features: [\"ellipsoid_volume\", \"dof1_std\", \"dof4_std\", \"dof5_std\", \"dof6_std\"]\n          dominant_dofs: [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]\n          interpretation: \"Noise affects all DOFs; CRM uses global ellipsoid volume + distributed per-DOF features\"\n        robot_state:\n          high_gradient_features: [\"dof1_conformal_r\", \"dof2_conformal_r\", \"dof3_conformal_r\", \"dof1_std\", \"dof2_std\"]\n          dominant_dofs: [1, 2, 3]\n          interpretation: \"Robot-state offset manifests as position bias; CRM focuses on position DOF conformal radii\"\n        light:\n          high_gradient_features: [\"ellipsoid_volume\", \"dof3_std\", \"dof5_std\", \"dof6_std\"]\n          dominant_dofs: [3, 5, 6]\n          interpretation: \"Lighting affects depth (z) and wrist rotation perception\"\n        gripper_gradient_ratio_vs_position: 0.31\n      top5_features_overall: [\"dof1_std\", \"dof2_std\", \"ellipsoid_volume\", \"dof3_conformal_r\", \"dof1_conformal_r\"]\n    result_rationale: >\n      CRM learns perturbation-specific attention patterns because different perturbation\n      types produce distinctive uncertainty signatures in the 45D feature space. Camera\n      perturbation inflates position DOF (1-3) uncertainties while leaving rotation/gripper\n      relatively stable — CRM correctly attends to position features. Noise uniformly\n      inflates all DOFs — CRM relies on the global ellipsoid volume as a summary statistic\n      plus distributed per-DOF features. Robot-state offset creates systematic position\n      bias detectable via conformal radii (which capture directional shift, not just\n      variance). The gripper DOF consistently receives low gradient magnitude (0.31× of\n      position DOFs) because gripper actions are discrete-like (open/close) and robust to\n      most perturbations. This perturbation-specific feature attention is the mechanistic\n      evidence for \"dimension-selective correction\" — CRM doesn't apply uniform corrections\n      but routes different perturbation types through different feature pathways.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 7: Top-5 Features by Gradient Magnitude per Perturbation\"\n        columns: [\"Perturbation\", \"Rank 1\", \"Rank 2\", \"Rank 3\", \"Rank 4\", \"Rank 5\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Camera\", \"dof1_std\", \"dof2_std\", \"dof3_std\", \"dof1_cr\", \"dof2_cr\"]\n          - [\"Noise\", \"ellipsoid_vol\", \"dof1_std\", \"dof4_std\", \"dof5_std\", \"dof6_std\"]\n          - [\"Robot-state\", \"dof1_cr\", \"dof2_cr\", \"dof3_cr\", \"dof1_std\", \"dof2_std\"]\n          - [\"Light\", \"ellipsoid_vol\", \"dof3_std\", \"dof5_std\", \"dof6_std\", \"dof3_cr\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Fig 9: 45×7 heatmap of normalized gradient magnitudes (features × perturbation types). Clear block-diagonal structure visible: position DOFs dominate for camera/robot-state, rotation DOFs for light, and distributed pattern for noise. This is the key mechanistic figure.\"\n      - \"Fig 9 inset: Gradient magnitude ratio (position DOFs / rotation DOFs) across perturbation types, showing >3× ratio for camera/robot-state and ~1× for noise.\"\n    analysis: >\n      The feature importance heatmap is arguably the most compelling figure in the paper.\n      The block-diagonal structure provides direct mechanistic evidence that CRM performs\n      dimension-selective correction — not as a design constraint but as an emergent behavior\n      from training on per-DOF conformal features. This figure should appear prominently\n      (main paper, not supplementary). It directly supports the \"first method to use\n      structured multivariate CP statistics as learned correction features\" positioning\n      by showing that the structure in the features is actually being exploited.\n\n  - name: \"SnapFlow Integration (K=10 × 1-step)\"\n    plan_id: \"plan_013\"\n    description: >\n      Replace 100-step flow-matching sampling with 1-step SnapFlow distilled generation.\n      K=10 samples generated via 1-step denoising. Test (a) sample diversity preservation\n      (measured by pairwise distance distribution compared to 100-step), (b) CGS feature\n      quality (covariance condition number), (c) downstream ACR-VLA performance, (d) wall-\n      clock inference time.\n    expected_results:\n      snapflow_sr_liberoplus: 72.5\n      snapflow_vs_full_gap_pp: 1.6\n      inference_time_snapflow_ms: 45\n      inference_time_full_ms: 385\n      speedup_factor: 8.6\n      sample_diversity_preservation_pct: 82\n      covariance_condition_number_snapflow: 22.3\n      covariance_condition_number_full: 14.7\n    result_rationale: >\n      1-step distilled generation preserves ~82% of sample diversity because SnapFlow's\n      consistency distillation maintains the marginal distribution but compresses the\n      sampling trajectory, losing some of the fine-grained stochasticity that 100-step\n      ODE integration explores. This manifests as slightly higher covariance condition\n      numbers (22.3 vs 14.7) — the covariance is still estimable but less well-conditioned\n      because sample diversity is reduced. The 1.6pp performance gap is modest because\n      Ledoit-Wolf shrinkage compensates for the slightly less stable covariance estimation,\n      and CRM is somewhat robust to covariance estimation noise (it learned smooth mappings\n      from noisy features during training with only K=10 samples). The 8.6× speedup (385ms\n      → 45ms) brings ACR-VLA inference into real-time territory for 10Hz control, making\n      it practically deployable. The speedup is slightly less than 100× (the sampling\n      step reduction) because CGS computation and CRM forward pass (together ~35ms)\n      dominate when sampling is fast.\n    tables:\n      - name: \"Table 8: SnapFlow Speed-Accuracy Tradeoff\"\n        columns: [\"Variant\", \"Sampling Steps\", \"Inference (ms)\", \"L-Plus SR%\", \"Δ vs Full\"]\n        rows:\n          - [\"Full (100-step)\", 100, 385, 74.1, \"—\"]\n          - [\"SnapFlow (1-step)\", 1, 45, 72.5, \"-1.6\"]\n    figures_description:\n      - \"Supplementary Fig: (Left) Pairwise sample distance distributions for 100-step vs 1-step, showing compressed but preserved distribution shape. (Right) Pareto frontier of inference time vs success rate, showing SnapFlow is near-optimal.\"\n    analysis: >\n      SnapFlow integration demonstrates practical viability: 1.6pp cost for 8.6× speedup\n      is an excellent tradeoff for real-time applications. The 82% diversity preservation\n      confirms that 1-step distilled sampling captures the bulk of epistemic uncertainty\n      even though it loses some of the tail diversity from full ODE integration. This\n      result should be presented as a deployment-oriented contribution rather than a core\n      scientific finding — it shows ACR-VLA's inference overhead can be made negligible\n      with off-the-shelf distillation.\n```","stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"3a8fe5df-a524-42fe-ad30-68007c28aa51","total_cost_usd":0.4477505,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11112,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"output_tokens":14648,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11112,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":14648,"cache_read_input_tokens":11841,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11112,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11112},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-haiku-4-5-20251001":{"inputTokens":6070,"outputTokens":20,"cacheReadInputTokens":0,"cacheCreationInputTokens":0,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.00617,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":32000},"claude-opus-4-6":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":14648,"cacheReadInputTokens":11841,"cacheCreationInputTokens":11112,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.4415805,"contextWindow":200000,"maxOutputTokens":64000}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","uuid":"00be863b-e941-4568-acb8-69321c01ee00"}
